We conclude this subsection with showing that, whenever $\cft$ and $\stgt$ are well-defined, they  coincide with their transfinite counterparts (proof in Appendix \ref{sec:appx for defining semantics}):

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{cftAndtfcftagree}
    \label{thm:when defined cf2 and icft agree}
    Let $\F$ be an AF and $S\subseteq A_\F$. Suppose that whether or not $S$ is a \cft-extension is well-defined. Then $S$ is a \cft-extension if and only if $S$ is a $\icft$-extension.
Similarly, suppose that whether or not $S$ is a \stgt-extension is well-defined. Then $S$ is an \stgt-extension if and only if $S$ is a \istgt-extension.
\end{restatable}






