\section{Discussion}

The results presented in this paper contribute to the broader understanding of argumentation in infinite domains, a setting of increasing relevance for AI systems that must operate over unbounded or dynamically evolving data. Our two proposed approaches---the transfinite extension of SCC-recursive semantics and the introduction of the \cfo and \stgo semantics---highlight different design trade-offs when generalizing well-understood concepts from the finite case.

The transfinite extensions provide a principled way to salvage the recursive methodology of \cft and \stgt in the infinite setting, aligning closely with known alternative characterizations. However, our analysis reveals that these extensions can suffer from foundational issues, particularly around directionality and existence, in infinite frameworks. %This underscores the challenge of transplanting finite constructions into infinite contexts without modification.

By contrast, the \cfo and \stgo semantics preserve the original SCC structure throughout the evaluation process. This approach avoids the need for transfinite recursion. Notably, in finitary AFs, \cfo and \stgo demonstrate superior adherence to the key evaluation criteria of directionality and existence, suggesting that they may serve as more robust alternatives for infinite argumentation reasoning tasks in practice.

% These findings also offer new insights into the structure of SCC-recursiveness itself. While originally intended as a strategy for managing cyclic interactions, our results indicate that its recursive decomposition mechanism—if not carefully constrained—can become problematic in the absence of finite bounds. This observation may inform future design principles for semantics in both finite and infinite settings.

Going forward, one promising direction is to study algorithmic properties and complexity results for the newly introduced semantics, particularly in the context of incremental or streaming argumentation systems. Additionally, we left open the question of finitary existence and directionality for the \icft semantics.
%Additionally, further investigation is warranted into the expressiveness and uniqueness of \cfo and \stgo. %may illuminate their relationship to other non-standard semantics, including those defined through fixed-point or game-theoretic constructions.

In sum, our work advances the theoretical foundations of infinite argumentation and provides practical tools for constructing semantics that remain meaningful in the presence of infinite interaction structures.






