 The preservation of symmetric equilibria and vansihing of asymmetric equilibria are illustrated below via an example.

 \begin{exmp}
     Consider $N$ users and all have linear $v$ except user $k$, which has a strict concave and differentiable $v$. Here, for any $\p\in\mathcal P_1$, we can see that all $\x\in X^*(\p)$ which satisfy $x^*_k=y_k$, are elements of $\widetilde X^*(\p)$. However, all $\x\in X^*(\p)$ with $x^*_k\ne y_k$ are not in $\widetilde X^*(\p)$. We illustrate this for the case $N=2$ in Fig. \ref{fig:destr-nash-eq}. There are two users, with corresponding variables $x_1$ and $x_2$. In the absence of any PT transformation, the set of Nash equilibria is  $X^*(\p)$, the blue line. If the first user has a PT type utility with a strictly concave transformation $v_1$, the only equilibrium  preserved is the point $\x(1)$; similarly, if the second user has a PT type utility with a strictly concave transformation $v_2$, the only equilibrium preserved is the point  $\x(2)$. If both users have strictly concave transformations $v_1$ and $v_2$, none of the equilibria are preserved, unless $\p=\overline\xi\textbf{1}$, in which case $\x(1)=\x(2)=\x=(y_1,y_2)$, and this point is preserved. For higher values of $N$, we will see a similar reduction of the dimension of the set of Nash equilibria, for every additional \lq irrational\rq~ user, leading to an empty set or a single point, depending on the price.
     \end{exmp}

Strictly convex transforms of the utility have the same effect.
\begin{prop}\label{prop:equilibrium-destr-cvx}
    If some $v_k$ is strictly convex, then any element of  $X^*(\p)$ which is asymmetric for user $k$, will not belong to $\widetilde X^*(\p)$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    The proof is the same as in Appendix \ref{sec:proof-equilibrium-destr}, with the change that $v_k^{\prime}$ here is strictly increasing. The appropriate changes in \eqref{eq:epsilon-zed}  lead to the result. 
\end{proof}

Propositions \ref{prop:equilibrium-destr} and \ref{prop:equilibrium-destr-cvx} suggest that transformations of the EUT utilities by functions which are strictly concave for gains and strictly convex for losses, do not preserve most of the equilibria of the EUT setting. However, the PT game may still have new Nash equilibria created by of the $v$ function. The existence and location of these equilibria will depend on the $v$ function, and the values of system parameters. Below, we show an example where for $\p=\overline\xi\textbf{1}$, and for a particular PT transformation function $v_i$, the PT game has no equilibria outside the set of the EUT equilibria.

\begin{exmp}\label{exmp:linear-deriv}
    Let $v_i$, for all $i$, have derivative
    $v_i^{\prime}(z)=cz+d$. If the parameters satisfy, for each $i$,
    $b_i-\overline\xi y_i<-\frac{d}{c},$
    we have $\widetilde X^*(\overline\xi\textbf{1})\subseteq X^*(\overline\xi\textbf{1})$.
\end{exmp}
The proof is provided in Appendix \ref{sec:proof-exmp-linear-deriv}. At this value of the parameter $\p$, using Proposition \ref{prop:equilibrium-PT}, we see that $|\widetilde X^*(\overline\xi \textbf 1)|=1$. Thus, the number of equilibria reduce from an uncountable set to a singleton, under the EUT to PT transformation.
