\section{How Prospect Theory Shapes Market Equilibria}\label{sec:numerical-study}
We adapt the game model presented in Section \ref{sec:GameModel} to an electricity market application, with the aim to analyze the impact of users' irrationality on the market equilibria. The EUT  game corresponds to the case where the users behave rationally, and the PT game represents the case where the users' behaviour departs from the rational model. 

The coordinator is an aggregator which serves a community of $N$ consumers (users), by buying electricity on the wholesale market, and proposing two-part tariffs. During the first part of the day, the consumers can plan ahead and purchase a certain quantity of electricity. For consumer $i$, this quantity is denoted by  $x_i$; the fixed fee (price) charged by the aggregator for this purchase is $p$ per unit. Let $\x$ denote the vector $(x_i)_{i=1}^N$, and $\p$ the vector $p\textbf{1}$.  The forecasted consumption of consumer $i$ is $y_i$; let $\y$ denote the vector $(y_i)_{i=1}^N$. If the consumption of consumers exceeds $x_i$, then consumer $i$ needs to buy the $y_i-x_i>0$ from the aggregator at a variable charge of $c+\xi$ where $c>0$ is a constant (e.g., peak electricity price from the wholesale market) and $\hat\xi$ is a random variable (e.g., due to the fluctuations of renewables). The variable in our model, $\xi$, is therefore equal to $c+\hat\xi$.  If the consumer $i$ uses less $y_i-x_i\leq 0$ than what it purchased, then it is refunded at the same variable charge.

The total cost paid by customer $i$ is a function that depends on their purchase, consumption, price of purchase and randomness from the renewables. It is in the interest of the consumers to have the total demand $\sum_iy_i$ to be close to the total units declared initially, $\sum_ix_i$.  We assume that the users are homogeneous, i.e., $a_i=a,b_i=b$  for all $i$. 

 We consider two candidates for the utility for the coordinator to maximize,
 \begin{align}
     \overline J_0^A(\p,\x) &=-||\x-\y||^2+||\p||^2,\\
     \overline J_0^B(\p,\x) &=\sum_{i=1}^N\overline J_i(x_i,\x_{-i},\p).
 \end{align}
 The first represents a symmetry plus revenue objective, while the second represents economic efficiency.
 \subsection{EUT Case}
 Since $a_i$ are equal, we see from \eqref{eqn:condtn-on-p} that
 $
     \mathcal P_1=\{p\textbf{1}:p\textbf{1}\in\mathcal P\}.
 $
 Define $\mathscr P=\{p:p\textbf{1}\in\mathcal P_1\}$, and let $\underline p$ and $\overline p$ be the maximal and minimal elements of this (compact) set. Using the form of the selected equilibrium \eqref{eq:selected-eq}, we obtain
 \begin{align*}
     \overline J_0^A(\p,\hat\x^{*}(\p))=-\frac{N^3}{4a^2}(p-\overline\xi)^2+ Np^2.
 \end{align*}
 For $N>2a$, this is maximized at
 $p^A_{\text{EUT}}=\mathrm{proj}_{\mathscr P}\left[\overline\xi\frac{1}{1-\frac{4a^2}{N^2}}\right].$
 Similarly we evaluate 
 \begin{align}
    \overline J_0^B(\p,\hat\x^{*}(\p)) &=(-\frac{N^3}{4a}+\frac{N^2}{2a})(p-\overline\xi)^2-p\sum_{i=1}^Ny_i,\\
     \frac{\partial^2 \overline J_0^B}{\partial p^2} &=-\frac{N^2}{a}(\frac{N}{2}-1)<0,~\text{for~}N>2.
 \end{align}
 Thus for $N>2$, the total utility is maximized when
$
     p^B_{\text{EUT}}=\mathrm{proj}_{\mathscr P}\left[\overline\xi-\frac{a\sum_{i=1}^Ny_i}{N^2(\frac{N}{2}-1)}\right].
 $
 For a large number of users (with $y_i$ taking values from a bounded set, or with appropriate growth conditions), both $p^A$ and $p^B$ are approximately equal  to $\overline\xi$.
 \subsection{PT Case}
 In the PT case, since we lack analytical expressions for the equilibria, we will use numerical simulations to obtain insights. We assume that the distortions are symmetric, i.e., $v_i=v$. 
Let us consider a simple example, with 2 users and with $M=2$. Let $a_1=a_2=0.5$, $b_1=b_2=10$, $\xi_1=10$, $\xi_2=50$, $q_1=0.25$, $q_2=0.75$, $y_1=7$, $y_2=4$.
 We consider two forms of the distortion function $v$, and obtain Nash equilibria using a user-wise gradient ascent (algorithm \ref{alg:con-alg-grad}), starting from random initializations.

 \noindent \textbf{Case 1}. We have $v(z)=1-\exp(-0.1z)$. This distortion is of the form in Theorem \ref{thm:uniq-PT-sym}. Consequently, at each price we will have a unique equilibrium. It can also be shown, using \cite[Theorem 5]{tan2023necessary} that for each Nash equilibrium of the PT game, there is a neighbourhood of the Nash equilibrium, such that gradient play starting in this neighbourhood, converges to this Nash equilibrium.

 The equilibria obtained by simulations for certain prices are depicted in Fig. \ref{fig:Nash-eq-creation-strict-ccv}, with the number next to the point indicating the price $p$. We observe that for both users, the value $x_i$ decreases as price increases. At the price $p=\overline\xi=40$, the equilibrium obtained is at $(y_1,y_2)=(7,4)$. Other than at this price, we do not recover any other EUT equilibrium at any other price. This is in line with Theorem \ref{thm:uniq-PT-sym}, and Propositions \ref{prop:equilibrium-PT} and \ref{prop:equilibrium-destr}. The equilibria obtained satisfy the structural condition obtained in Theorem \ref{thm:uniq-PT-sym},
 \begin{align}\label{eq:dashed-line}
     y_1-x_1=y_2-x_2,
 \end{align}
 which is represented by the dashed line. Numerically, with the price $p$ being restricted to the set $[1,50]$, we obtain the prices that maximize $\overline J_0^A$ and $\overline J_0^B$ as
$$p^A_{\text{PT}}=\overline p=50,p^B_{\text{PT}}=\underline p=1$$ respectively. The corresponding equilibria are indicated with a red circle and a black circle, respectively. Recall that in this case $p^A_{\text{EUT}}=\mathrm{proj}_{\mathscr P}[\frac{4}{3}\overline\xi]$. If $\overline p<\frac{4}{3}\overline\xi$, we will have $p^A_{\text{PT}}=p^A_{\text{EUT}}$. If $\overline p\ge \frac{4}{3}\overline\xi$, then $p^A_{\text{PT}}\ge p^A_{\text{EUT}}$. Irrational behaviour of the customers leads to a higher overall pricing by the coordinator, if they use the utility  $\overline J_0^A$. While $p^B_{\text{EUT}}$ is not available for $N=2$, we see that for small $N$, $p^B_{\text{EUT}}<\overline\xi$. The value of $\underline p$ may be even lower, which suggests that irrational preferences lower the price, if the coordinator uses the utility $\overline J_0^B$. Thus, if the coordinator uses the metric $\overline J_0^A$, which takes into account symmetry and revenue, irrationality results in an increase in prices. On the other hand, if the coordinator uses the utility $\overline J_0^B$, which is the economic efficiency, irrationality leads to a decrease in prices.
