\section{Introduction}
Point cloud surface representation is a fundamental technique in computer graphics, computer vision, and 3D modeling \cite{li2016sparse}. 
Among the various representation paradigms, signed distance functions (SDFs) have gained widespread adoption due to their ability to implicitly and compactly encode complex surface geometries as the zero-level set of a continuous function \cite{calakli2011ssd, park2019deepsdf}. 
This representation not only facilitates surface reconstruction but also enables efficient storage, editing, and physical simulation.
Despite their advantages, SDF-based methods face an inherent trade-off between representation fidelity and computational efficiency. High-fidelity SDF approximations typically require dense sampling or a large number of basis functions, which incurs significant memory and computation costs. On the other hand, sparse approximations often fail to capture fine geometric detail, especially in the presence of anisotropic features.

Classical implicit surface methods, including radial basis function (RBF) interpolation \cite{carr2001reconstruction, dinh2002reconstructing, turk2002modelling, ohtake2006sparse, samozino2006reconstruction} and Poisson surface reconstruction \cite{kazhdan2006Poisson, kazhdan2013screened, kazhdan2020poisson}, represent surfaces as the zero-level set of a fitted continuous function. These techniques are robust to noise and produce smooth, watertight surfaces, yet they suffer from two major limitations: (1) conventional isotropic RBFs inefficiently model directional or elongated surface structures, often requiring excessive basis functions to fit complex geometry; (2) the dense solvers typically employed in these frameworks scale poorly with increasing data size, limiting their practical usability in large-scale scenarios. More recently, learning-based methods have introduced neural networks for SDF representation \cite{guerrero2018pcpnet, Erler_2020, gropp2020implicit, erler2024ppsurf}. While these approaches improve generalization and surface quality, they rely on computationally expensive training pipelines and lack explicit control over the sparsity and structure of the learned representation. 

In this paper, motivated by the success of ellipsoidal radial basis functions (ERBFs) in molecular surface representation \cite{gui2020molecular} and their use in Gaussian splatting \cite{kerbl20233d}, we propose the Sparse Ellipsoidal Radial Basis Function Network (SE-RBFNet), a fast and efficient machine learning approach for representing point cloud surfaces by approximating SDFs.
Our method shares similarities with SparseRBF \cite{li2016sparse}, which also employs RBFs for the sparse representation of point cloud surfaces. There are two important differences between the two methods.
First, SparseRBF typically utilizes spherical basis functions, optimizing only the weight coefficients. This design aims to transform the problem into a linear Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator (LASSO) \cite{tibshirani1996regression} problem, thereby accelerating the optimization process but lacking the flexibility to accurately fit complex surfaces. 
In contrast, SE-RBFNet adopts ERBFs and performs joint optimization of the center positions, the rotation angles along the principal axes, axis lengths, and the weight coefficients. This is a high dimensional nonlinear nonconvex optimization problem. The proposed comprehensive optimization scheme significantly enhances the model's capacity to represent complex geometric features while requiring a much smaller number of basis functions.
The second difference lies in the initialization of the center points. SparseRBF positions the center points along the central axis of the point cloud object, whereas SE-RBFNet uses an inscribed sphere strategy to initialize the center points, improving the adaptability to various surface structures.

In addition, we initially applied radial basis networks for sparse representation in the image domain \cite{chen2020sparse}, and later extended this approach to 3D point cloud surfaces \cite{wang2021point}. 
Compared to our earlier work, SE-RBFNet incorporates a multi-objective optimization strategy  \cite{sener2018multi},  which adaptively balances L2 error term and L1 regularization term of the basis coefficients to achieve an optimal trade-off between representation accuracy and sparsity. 
To further improve computational efficiency and robustness, a nearest-neighbor search method is used to identify points near the center of each Gaussian kernel during both the forward and backward steps of optimization, effectively reducing computational complexity.
The optimization process is further accelerated via CUDA-based parallel computation.
Moreover, we design a hierarchical optimization strategy based on octree structures \cite{wilhelms1992octrees}. 
Specifically, the initialization and optimization of ERBF parameters are first conducted using coarse grid points in the octree lattice structure. As the optimization progresses, finer lattice points are progressively incorporated into the training set. In addition, extreme error points—identified based on the L2 error at grid points—are introduced as new kernel centers. This hierarchical and iterative process not only improves parameter accuracy but also effectively  accelerates the convergence of the training process.

We summarize our contributions as follows. 
\begin{itemize} 
    \item SE-RBFNet is proposed, where the point cloud surface representation problem is reformulated as a nonlinear sparse optimization problem (see Section \ref{subsection:Ellipsoid RBF Neural Network}). 
    \item A dynamic multi-objective optimization strategy is introduced to balance approximation accuracy and sparsity (see Section \ref{subsection:loss_function}). 
    \item A coarse-to-fine hierarchical optimization strategy based on octree lattice structure is developed, and a nearest-neighbor filtering mechanism is incorporated to improve efficiency and reduce model complexity (see Section \ref{subsubsection:Hierarchical Optimization}). 
    \item An adaptive basis function addition mechanism is designed, in which new kernel centers are iteratively selected from high-error regions and ellipsoidal parameters are dynamically updated, enabling improved accuracy and faster convergence. (see Section \ref{subsubsection:Adaptive Basis Function Adjustment}). 
\end{itemize}



\section{Related Work}
Surface representation from point clouds has been a longstanding topic of research, with implicit representations being particularly effective in capturing both local geometry and global surface topology. In this section, we briefly review classical and learning-based implicit methods.


\subsection{Classical Implicit Representations}
\label{subsection:Classical Implicit Methods}
Classical implicit methods typically represent the surface as the zero-level set of a continuous function, often a SDF. This strategy enables flexible surface representation and efficient reconstruction via methods like the Marching Cubes algorithm \cite{lorensen1998marching}.

Early work by Curless and Levoy \cite{curless1996volumetric} introduced volumetric SDF fusion, laying the foundation for subsequent approaches. 
Calakli and Taubin \cite{calakli2011ssd} further emphasized that the target implicit function should approximate the true SDF, rather than merely filling volume. 
Pan and Skala \cite{pan2011continuous} proposed a continuous global optimization framework that minimizes a variational energy combining data fidelity and smoothness, enabling robust surface reconstruction from noisy or incomplete oriented point clouds.
In a subsequent work, Pan and Skala \cite{pan2012surface} improved upon their previous approach by incorporating higher-order derivative regularization into the optimization, further enhancing surface smoothness.
Poisson surface reconstruction (PSR) and its variants \cite{kazhdan2006Poisson, kazhdan2013screened, sellan2022stochastic} transform reconstruction into solving a spatial Poisson equation, generating smooth and globally consistent surfaces. Building upon the PSR, Hou et al. \cite{hou2022iterative} proposed an iterative PSR method that estimates normals from reconstructed surfaces in each iteration, progressively enhancing surface quality. 


RBFs are another popular choice for implicit surface modeling. These methods place an RBF at each point and optimize the corresponding weights to fit the surface. 
Carr et al. \cite{carr2001reconstruction} proposed multi-harmonic RBFs for smooth surface interpolation, and introduced a greedy algorithm, which iteratively added centers associated with large residuals, reducing the total number of basis functions.
Ohtake et al. \cite{ohtake2006sparse} decomposed the global approximation problem into overlapping local subproblems, each solved via least-squares RBF fitting. This significantly reduces the number of required basis functions, enabling sparse yet efficient surface representation.
Samozino et al. \cite{samozino2006reconstruction} proposed selecting RBF centers directly from the Voronoi vertices computed from the input point cloud, rather than placing them on the surface or its offset. This strategy yields a more uniform spatial distribution of centers. However, since the fitting process treats both surface and off-surface points equally, approximation errors near the true surface can increase. 
Further developments on RBF-based surface modeling have explored anisotropic kernels \cite{casciola2006shape}, compactly supported RBFs \cite{walder2006implicit, pan2011two}, orthogonal least squares (OLS) center selection \cite{xia2006orthogonal}, and Hermite RBFs, which directly incorporate normal information \cite{brazil2010sketching, macedo2011hermite, liu2016closed}. 
In large-scale contexts such as geographic surface modeling, space-partitioned and comparative studies \cite{majdisova2017big} have demonstrated both the potential and the limitations of conventional RBFs in handling massive point sets efficiently.
More recently, RBF-based surface reconstruction has been further extended through sparse center selection \cite{wang2021point}, partitioned formulations \cite{drake2022implicit}, and novel interpolation schemes \cite{zeng2022implicit}, broadening its applicability across different surface fitting scenarios.

Beyond RBFs, other implicit representations include Implicit Moving Least Squares (IMLS) \cite{shen2004interpolating, fuhrmann2014floating}, Fourier bases \cite{Kazhdan_2005}, and Gaussian formulations \cite{lu2018surface, lin2022surface}. These approachs further highlight the versatility and robustness of implicit methods in surface representation.
Another important family is implicit moving least squares (IMLS), first proposed by Shen et al. \cite{shen2004interpolating}. IMLS fits a local weighted least squares plane around each query point, constructing an implicit function whose zero-level set defines the surface. To improve scalability and adaptivity for large-scale point clouds, subsequent works have introduced enhancements such as adaptive neighborhood selection \cite{dey2005adaptive}, provably correct projections \cite{kolluri2008provably}, floating scale support \cite{fuhrmann2014floating}, and deep learning-based acceleration \cite{liu2021deep}.


\subsection{Neural Implicit Representations}
\label{subsection:Neural Implicit Representations}
Recent methods leverage neural networks to learn implicit shape representations. Mescheder et al. \cite{mescheder2019occupancy} introduced Occupancy Networks, parameterizing implicit functions via neural networks and latent codes. Park et al. \cite{park2019deepsdf} proposed DeepSDF, which represents shapes as continuous SDFs modeled by multilayer perceptrons (MLPs). Peng et al. \cite{peng2020convolutional} further extended this idea by leveraging 3D convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to capture shape priors.

While these methods achieve high reconstruction quality, they often rely on curated datasets and struggle to generalize to unseen shapes.
To address this, Gropp et al. \cite{gropp2020implicit} incorporated geometric regularization to enhance  generalization. Ben-Shabat et al. \cite{ben2022digs} introduced soft second-order constraints, and Wang et al. \cite{wang2023neural} minimized the Hessian determinant near surfaces to suppress artifacts. 

In contrast to global shape models, Points2Surf \cite{Erler_2020} and PPSurf \cite{erler2024ppsurf} focus on local, patch-based learning with self-supervision, enhancing robustness to noise, sparsity, and varying sampling density. Ma et al. \cite{ma2020neural} further relaxed supervision requirements by proposing an unsupervised learning framework that estimates surface geometry directly from point clouds without ground-truth SDFs.

These studies highlight the importance of SDFs as a fundamental representation of surfaces, which motivates our adoption of sparse ellipsoidal RBF networks for efficient SDF approximation.

Our work follows the learning-based SDF approximation paradigm by adopting a sparse ellipsoidal RBF network to approximate the SDF, thereby representing the implicit surface as a combination of ellipsoids. 
Compared to traditional RBF-based approaches, our method formulates a nonlinear optimization problem by optimizing both kernel centers and shapes. It incorporates ellipsoidal anisotropy, sparse center selection, and hierarchical optimization to enhance both efficiency and accuracy.
Rather than directly focusing on surface reconstruction, our method aims to construct a compact and sparse representation of the SDF, from which the point cloud surface can be naturally recovered as the level set.


