\section{Conclusions and Future Works}
%The proposed zero-touch network platform for smart factories significantly advances the objectives of Industry 4.0 by integrating a hierarchical O-RAN architecture and leveraging federated deep reinforcement learning (FedDRL). FedDRL enhances long-term system performance and transmission efficiency by deploying the global D3QN model in transmitters that further optimized with a prioritized experience replay mechanism and momentum-optimized stochastic gradient descent. Extensive simulations demonstrate a 10\% improvement in system throughput and a 32\% increase in normalized average transmission efficiency compared to the baseline IDRL, while achieving a 30\% reduction in average transmission energy consumption.

%This paper introduces a zero-touch network platform for smart factory applications, integrating the hierarchical O-RAN architecture with federated deep reinforcement learning (FedDRL). The proposed platform enables parameter reconfiguration of transmitters through the use of advanced FedDRL technique, achieving significant improvements in system throughput, transmission energy efficiency, and transmission energy consumption. The results demonstrate the potential of this platform to advance industry 4.0 objectives by optimizing the performance of multirobot system and addressing the complexities of dynamic and heterogeneous factory environments.
This paper presents a zero-touch network platform designed for smart factory applications, integrating a hierarchical Open Radio Access Network architecture with federated deep reinforcement learning (FedDRL). The introduced platform enables intelligent parameter reconfiguration of multirobot systems by leveraging FedDRL-driven adaptive transmitter optimization, specifically through modulation and coding scheme selection and transmission power control. By federating learning across distributed agents, the platform enhances system throughput, transmission energy efficiency, and overall transmission performance in dynamic and heterogeneous factory environments. The research findings highlight the platform’s potential to advance Industry 4.0 objectives by ensuring seamless data-driven adaptation in complex industrial ecosystems.

%Future works will focus on several key directions to extend the capabilities of the proposed platform. First, the integration of additional machine learning models, such as unsupervised and semi-supervised learning techniques, will be explored to enhance adaptability to diverse factory scenarios with limited labeled data. Second, multi-agent collaboration mechanisms will be investigated to further optimize resource allocation and decision making across distributed agents. Third, real-world deployment and testing in industrial environments will be prioritized to evaluate the practical feasibility and robustness of the platform. Finally, extending the platform to support ultra-low latency and high-reliability applications, such as tele-operated robotics and augmented reality systems, will be a critical area for future development.
%Future research will focus on extending the capabilities of the proposed platform in several key directions. First, the integration of additional machine learning models, such as unsupervised and semi-supervised learning techniques will be explored to enhance adaptability to diverse factory scenarios, particularly in situations with limited labeled data. Additionally, real-world deployment and testing in industrial environments will be prioritized to assess the platform's practical feasibility and robustness. Lastly, further development will focus on extending the platform’s support for ultra-low latency and high-reliability applications to meet the stringent requirements of next-generation industrial automation.

Expanding beyond the current single-edge-cloud assumption, future research will investigate a multi-edge-cloud environment, explicitly addressing the mobility of transmitters across multiple near-real-time RICs (nRT-RICs). This extension will clarify how the proposed system adapts as transmitters transition between the control domains of different nRT-RICs, thus capturing the complexities introduced by dynamic wireless conditions and interactions among multiple access points of the different nRT-RICs. Additionally, real-world deployment and comprehensive testing in industrial settings will be emphasized to validate the practicality and scalability of the proposed platform under diverse operational scenarios.

