\section{Introduction}

Large Language Models (LLMs) have been shown to memorize information from their training data~\citep{nasr2023scalable,carlini2021extracting}, which often includes web-crawled content from a wide range of public and semi-public sources. This raises significant privacy concerns for data subjects whose personal information may be unintentionally retained and revealed. To mitigate these risks, LLMs typically undergo an alignment phase~\citep{rafailov2023direct,peng2023instruction}, during which they are fine-tuned to adhere to safety and privacy guidelines. Despite these efforts, recent research~\citep{liu2023autodan,chao2023jailbreaking,mehrotra2024tree} has demonstrated that LLMs can be jailbroken—i.e., prompted or manipulated to bypass alignment constraints and generate restricted outputs. However, most existing benchmarks~\citep{mazeika2024harmbench,souly2024strongreject} emphasize harmfulness or copyright violations, without focus on the leakage of personally identifiable information (PII) tied to specific individuals.

In this work, we investigate privacy jailbreaking in LLMs via \textit{steering}. Our attack focuses on \textit{public figures} as data subjects and \textit{sexual orientation} as the private attribute, chosen for its  verifiability and sensitivity. We aim to answer two key questions: (1) Can aligned LLMs be steered to produce non-refusal responses to privacy-sensitive prompts that probe PIIs? (2) If so, do the resulting responses disclose factual personal information or merely hallucinate? Our approach first identifies attention heads whose activations are predictive of refusal behavior by training lightweight linear probes. We then intervene on a subset of these heads to steer the model’s output toward disclosure. This setup avoids the use of attacker LLMs and instead directly modifies internal activations of the target model.

Our experiments yield two key findings. \textbf{First}, attention head activations can reliably predict binary refusal/disclosure behavior given prompts, and steering the top-$k$ heads induces non-refusal responses to sensitive queries. \textbf{Second}, we find that these steered responses often align with factual personal information, revealing real-world details such as relationships and personal events that would otherwise be refused. Overall, this study highlights a critical privacy risk: sensitive information memorized during pretraining can be extracted by intervening in internal model representations of the aligned LLMs. This underscores the need for more rigorous privacy testing by LLM providers.




\section{Related Work}\label{sec:rwork}

\noindent \textbf{Privacy Leakage Assessment.}  
Previous benchmarks for privacy leakage evaluation, such as TrustLLM~\citep{sun2024trustllm} and Decoding Trust~\citep{wang2023decodingtrust}, primarily focus on the leakage of {email addresses} in the Enron Email dataset~\citep{shetty2004enron}, which is part of the PILE corpus~\citep{gao2020pile}. However, email PII is often sanitized using regular expressions during pretraining, and the strict assumption that LLMs are pretrained on Enron subjects makes these benchmarks less effective for assessing real-world leakage. In contrast, our study shifts the focus to the leakage of sensitive sexual orientation information about public figures, who are often included in pretraining data from diverse sources.

\noindent \textbf{LLM Jailbreaking.}  
Numerous jailbreaking techniques have been proposed, including prompt-based attacks~\citep{li2023multi} using auxiliary LLMs~\citep{chao2023jailbreaking, mehrotra2024tree} and linguistic perturbations~\citep{liu2023autodan}. Among these, LLM steering has emerged as a compelling paradigm for analyzing model behavior from a mechanistic perspective. While prior work has applied steering to elicit unsafe or policy-violating content~\citep{cao2025scans, cao2024nothing,li2023multi,kirch2024features}, our work uniquely focuses on using targeted LLM steering to probe privacy leakage of data subjects. 

