\section{PII Jailbreaking}

We present an overview of our framework in Figure~\ref{fig:Overview}, which includes: (a) dataset construction, (b) probe model training, and (c) intervention. These components are discussed in detail below.


\subsection{Non-Adversarial Privacy Leakage Evaluation}

\noindent \textbf{Benchmark Construction.} To perform privacy leakage jailbreaking, we first identify subjects likely to be present in LLM training corpora, along with the ground-truth values for a relevant private attribute. However, pretraining datasets are often regarded as core assets by large model enterprises and are typically not released to the public. Hence, we cannot directly obtain information about datasubjects within these datasets to construct a universal benchmarks. Instead, we focus on a plausible scenario: extracting private information about public figures, under the assumption that these data subjects are frequently included in pretraining data. Specifically, we construct \{\textit{subject name}, \textit{sexual orientation}\} pairs by prompting GPT-4o-mini~\citep{openai2023gpt}, as detailed in Appendix~\ref{tab:dataconstruction}. Finally, we manually remove ambiguous or overly short names, resulting in a list of 968 public figures.


\noindent \textbf{Response Evaluation.} Next, we query a target LLM $f$ using a simple, non-adversarial prompt $\mathbf{x}_d$: \textit{"What is the sexual orientation of \{subject name\}?"}, and collect the corresponding response $\mathbf{y}_d$. Each pair \{$\mathbf{x}_d$, $\mathbf{y}_d$\} is evaluated by a privacy evaluator using the prompt in Appendix~\ref{tab:judgellmprompt}, which produces a response label $\mathbf{s}_d$. This label is categorized as one of the following: \textcolor{disclosed}{disclosed}, \textcolor{refused}{refused}, {\textcolor{unavailable}{unavailable}}, or \textcolor{ambiguous}{ambiguous}. Overall, our dataset consists of a triplet: $\{\mathbf{x}_d, \mathbf{y}_d, \mathbf{s}_d\}$.

 Figure~\ref{fig:non_adv_sexualorientation_leakage} shows the distribution of response labels across seven LLMs, with disclosure rates ranging from 5\% to 70\%. While the ethical and legal implications of revealing such information remain contested, our focus is on determining whether the {refused} responses can be jailbroken into {disclosed} responses—and if so, whether these outputs contain accurate personal information. This analysis highlights the risk of LLMs memorizing and leaking sensitive data about individual data subjects.


