\section{Experimental Results}\label{sec:experiments}

\noindent \textbf{Target Models.} We consider four LLM models for our experiments. The results using Llama2-7B~\citep{touvron2023llama} are presented in the main paper, while the results for Qwen7B~\citep{qwen}, Gemma9B~\citep{team2024gemma} and GLM9B~\citep{glm2024chatglm} are deferred to the Appendix due to space limitations. We apply interventions on top-$k$ attention heads, where $k \in \{16, 32, 48, 64, 96\}$, and $\alpha$ varies from $-80$ to $30$ in the steps of $10$. We use Gemma9B~\citep{team2024gemma} as our privacy evaluator. Further experimental details can be found in the Appendix.




\subsection{Probes Predict Privacy Refusal Behavior}

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:proberesultsauc}, we first evaluate the efficacy of the trained probes by analyzing the activation of each attention head. We observe that the middle layers of the LLMs are more effective than the initial and later layers. For instance, in the LLaMa7B~\citep{touvron2023llama}, the best-performing probe achieves AUCROC and F1 scores of 0.89 and 0.83, respectively, on the validation set. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of localizing privacy refusal behavior within LLMs when prompted.

\subsection{Steering LLMs to Privacy Jailbreak}
Next, we consider the 830 data subjects that constitute $D_{test}$, whose responses $y_d$ to the given prompt $\mathbf{x}_d$, without any steering, exhibit behavior other than \textit{disclosed}, as evaluated by the privacy evaluator.

 We then generate steered responses to the same prompt $\mathbf{x}_d$ by shifting the activation of the top-$k$ attention heads with strength $\alpha$, as shown in Equation~\ref{eq:eq1}. In Figure~\ref{fig:proberesults} (a), we present a heatmap illustrating the number of data subjects in $D_{test}^{R}$ whose responses, when evaluated by the privacy evaluator, are labeled as \textit{disclosed}. We observe that, among the tested hyperparameters, $k=96$ and $\alpha=-70$ lead to the highest disclosure rate, with 589 subjects disclosed from a total of 830. However, the optimal values for $\alpha$ and $k$ vary for different models, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:steeringresultsallmodels} in Appendix. Moreover, we find that the probability of disclosing at least once across all 66 steering combinations for each data subject is \textbf{97\%}. This suggests that steering serves as a effective attack method to induce non-refusal behaviors in LLMs when responding to privacy-probing prompts.

\subsection{Do Jailbroken Responses Contain Real Privacy Leakage?}

While steering can induce non-refusal behavior in LLMs, our primary goal is to determine whether the responses labeled as \textit{disclosed} by the privacy evaluator actually contain verifiable personal information about the data subjects. To assess factual accuracy, we use GPT-4~\citep{openai2023gpt} with the prompt provided in Appendix~\ref{tab:factualevaluationprompt}, classifying steered responses into one of four categories: \textit{Factual}, \textit{Hallucination}, \textit{Speculative}, or \textit{Inaccurate}. Notably, the factuality evaluator considers the entire generated response—which often includes information beyond sexual orientation—to make a holistic judgment on whether the generated content is factually accurate. Additionally, the factuality evaluator is also instructed within the prompt to produce a concise reasoning and enumerate the verifiable personal information included in the response. 

In Figure~\ref{fig:proberesults} (b), we plot the count of \textit{disclosed} steered responses  in $D_{test}$ that contain true information about probed \textit{sexual orientation} information. In Figure~\ref{fig:proberesults} (c), we show the number of responses containing hallucinated information. As observed, the steered responses frequently reveal factual information about many data subjects, leading to the leakage of personal information. For example, with steering parameters top-$k=96$ and $\alpha=-70$, the number of responses labeled as \textit{disclosed} is $589$, of which $339$ are factually correct, while $224$ are classified as hallucinated content. We provide demonstration examples of the steered content in Tables~\ref{fig:demoexample1} and~\ref{fig:demoexample2} in the Appendix. This experiment demonstrates that we can uncover memorized information about data subjects by steering the LLMs. 





\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we investigate privacy jailbreaking by steering the activations of attribute-discriminative attention heads using lightweight probes. We demonstrate that such steering leads LLMs to generate responses that not only bypass refusal behaviour but also disclose factual, personal information about data subjects. Overall, our findings highlight that privacy-related prompts with targeted steering can serve as a stress test for assessing the extent of memorization of personal information in LLMs.


\section{Limitations}

Our work has several limitations. First, we rely on automatic privacy evaluation via prompting, but the use of LLM-as-a-Judge~\cite{gu2024survey} introduces inherent biases and can lead to labeling errors. Second, the factual accuracy of steered responses is assessed using GPT-4~\citep{openai2023gpt}, which may also produce evaluation errors. In future work, we aim to address these issues by developing more robust privacy evaluators and leveraging agents with web access to verify factual claims. Finally, our analysis is limited to a single private attribute; extending this framework to other sensitive and verifiable attributes remains an important direction for future research.



\section{Broader Impact and Ethics Statement}Our work could potentially be exploited by attackers to probe and extract personal information about data subjects. However, the primary motivation behind this research is to demonstrate a deep understanding of the memorization capabilities within LLMs. By uncovering the memorized information about data subjects, we aim to contribute to efforts that safeguard the rights of these individuals. We curate the data subjects by prompting GPT-4 using prompt in Appendix~\ref{tab:dataconstruction}. Furthermore, for ethical reasons, we anonymize both the data subject names and the LLM responses.

