\section{Implementation Details}

We use pretrained, instruction-tuned models from HuggingFace~\citep{wolf2019huggingface}. For response generation, we allow a maximum of 200 tokens and apply greedy decoding for the target LLM. For the privacy evaluator, we employ top-$k$ sampling with $k=1$, as we observed that greedy decoding did not consistently produce outputs in the expected structured format.


\section{Additional Results}

In Tables ~\ref{fig:demoexample1}, ~\ref{fig:demoexample2} and ~\ref{fig:demoexample3}, we provide demonstration examples with and without steering. In Figure 6, we present the results of the probes for different LLMs using three metrics: ROC score, F1 score, and accuracy score, evaluated on the balanced validation set of 56 samples. \\

Next, we select 830, 440, 330, and 387 subjects that are labeled other than \textit{disclosed} by the privacy evaluator to prompt without any steering. These form Dtest for LLaMa7B~\citep{touvron2023llama}, Qwen7B~\citep{qwen}, Gemma~\citep{team2024gemma}, and GLM9B~\citep{glm2024chatglm} , respectively. In Figure 7, we show the count of disclosed responses in Dtest with steering on the left, the number of factually verifiable ones in the middle, and the number of hallucinated ones on the right. Moreover, we find that the probability of disclosing at least once across all 66 steering combinations for each data subject is 97.6\%, 99.2\%, 69.3\%, and 83.2\% for LLaMA7B, Qwen7B, Gemma9B, and GLM9B, respectively. Furthermore, among the steered responses labeled as disclosed by the privacy evaluator, around 50\% are found to be factually correct, as evaluated using GPT-4.


