The simulation results clearly demonstrate the advantages of the HERMES memory hierarchy over the baseline RISC-V system:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Latency Reduction}: HERMES reduces latency by up to 33\% through shared L3 caches, advanced prefetching, and tensor-aware caching.
    \item \textbf{Bandwidth Improvement}: The hybrid memory model increases bandwidth by up to 68\%, supporting the high data transfer needs of ML workloads.
    \item \textbf{Cache Efficiency}: Tensor-aware caching improves cache hit rates by 50\%, reducing the need for expensive off-chip memory accesses.
    \item \textbf{Energy Efficiency}: HERMES reduces energy consumption by up to 30\%, making it suitable for energy-efficient ML computation.
\end{itemize}

These improvements highlight the effectiveness of integrating advanced memory design techniques into RISC-V architectures to meet the demands of modern ML workloads.

