\begin{abstract}
% Large language models (LLMs) have driven significant advancements, becoming essential to modern deep learning (DL) applications. LLMs rely on DL frameworks for efficient model construction, distributed execution, and optimized deployment across diverse platforms.
% Poor usability and limited functionality of DL frameworks hinder LLM development efficiency and adaptability for users, while latent bugs can cause severe failures or resource waste due to the models’ large scale and long training cycles.
% % Therefore, investigating the LLM-related challenges is critical to the quality assurance of DL frameworks nowadays.
% % Unfortunately, existing work mainly addresses bugs in the design and architecture of LLM itself, neglecting the challenges from DL frameworks that harm LLM. As a result, it faces three key limitations.
% Therefore, understanding LLM-related challenges is essential for the quality assurance of DL frameworks. However, existing research mainly focuses on LLM model-level issues, overlooking the framework-side challenges that significantly impact LLM reliability, leading to three key limitations.
% (1) Neglect LLM-related framework usability.
% LLM requires increased user domain knowledge, escalating demands the DL frameworks such as usability and efficiency. Studying user challenges in LLM development and developer hurdles for issue resolution promotes framework optimization. However, such investigation remains under-examined.
% (2) Overlook LLM-related framework bug analysis. 
% Understanding bug symptoms and root causes is key to effective testing and debugging. Analyzing severity and triggers helps prioritize critical bugs, yet no existing work has established a taxonomy of LLM-related framework bugs.
% (3) Lack of LLM-oriented testing techniques. 
% There is no customized method for addressing such bugs, while the effectiveness of current DL framework testing methods in detecting LLM-related bugs remains unverified. A systematic evaluation is needed to understand their limitations and explore potential enhancements.
% To address these gaps, we conduct a three-stage empirical study. Firstly, we analyze issue reports from four mainstream LLM-supported DL frameworks (i.e., MindSpore, PyTorch, TensorFlow) and eight LLM acceleration libraries to extract LLM-related user demands, questions, and framework bugs. Based on these findings, we conduct targeted interviews with 11 LLM users and eight DL framework developers to validate the initial results and gain deeper insights.
% Finally, guided by the concluded insights, we revisit ten state-of-the-art DL framework testing methods to assess their effectiveness in detecting LLM-related bugs, uncover key limitations, and propose practical improvements.
% Our findings span three areas: (1) insights for users and developers on using DL frameworks for LLM development, (2) characteristics of LLM-related bugs, and (3) optimization suggestions for existing methods.\looseness=-1 
Large language models (LLMs) drive significant advancements in real industry applications. LLMs rely on DL frameworks for efficient model construction, distributed execution, and optimized deployment. Their large parameter scale and long execution cycles place extreme demands on DL frameworks in terms of scalability, stability, and efficiency. Therefore, poor usability, limited functionality, and subtle bugs in DL frameworks may hinder development efficiency and cause severe failures or resource waste.
However, a fundamental question remains under-investigated, i.e., \textbf{What challenges do DL frameworks face in supporting LLMs?} 
% Identifying missing features, usability gaps, and frequent bugs helps developers prioritize upgrades, improve framework quality, and expand adoption. Understanding the challenges in diagnosing and resolving LLM-related issues also informs the design of more effective testing and debugging techniques.
To seek an answer, we investigate these challenges through a large-scale analysis of issue reports from three major DL frameworks (MindSpore, PyTorch, TensorFlow) and eight associated LLM toolkits (e.g., Megatron). We construct a taxonomy of LLM-centric bugs, requirements, and user questions and enrich it through interviews with 11 LLM users and eight DL framework developers, uncovering key technical challenges and misalignments between user needs and developer priorities. Our contributions are threefold: (1) we develop a comprehensive taxonomy comprising four question themes (nine sub-themes), four requirement themes (15 sub-themes), and ten bug themes (45 sub-themes); (2) we assess the perceived importance and priority of these challenges based on practitioner insights; and (3) we identify five key findings across the LLM development and propose five actionable recommendations to improve the reliability, usability, and testability of DL frameworks.
Our results highlight critical limitations in current DL frameworks and offer concrete guidance for advancing their support for the next generation of LLM construction and applications.


\end{abstract}






