$100\,ms$ and $200\,ms$ (hashing random data) before writing each chunk.
In this case, CAWL takes the ongoing background flush during the computation
into account and still estimates the IO costs reasonably well.

CAWL does not only take the computations before IO into account, but also considers the randomness of IO.
\Cref{fig:async_random} compares the values of CAWL, SimGrid, and the experimental
results in the case of random IO when having the following
situations before writing each subsequent data chunk:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\Alph*)]
