\subsection{ML Classifier and Baseline Results} \label{5b}
%Given that the only kind of features we are using for our predictions is text sentiments, we believe that these results are very encouraging. Most papers we encountered in the literature that explore the effect of features on their predictions, always use them together with previous prices (or price movements) of the asset they are trying to predict. The reason of course is that due to the high autocorrelation of past prices ML models greatly improve their error metrics. However, in that way, the stand-alone predictive power of the features under investigation isn’t being demonstrated. Thus, by removing the “past price” factor (and any other feature irrelevant to the sentiment) in our analysis we try to quantify as clearly as possible the individual contribution of sentiment in our predictions. Probably if we used a greater variety of input features (e.g. traded volumes, past prices, Google Trend Analytics for cryptos, sentiment from social media etc.) we would see a much greater improvement in our metrics. However, the focus of this thesis is to serve as a proof of concept for the discovery and quantification of the predictive power of the arbitrary embedded text content inside the blockchains and how NLP techniques can be used to that end.
