\firstsection{Introduction}

\maketitle

Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) are AI systems capable of understanding and generating content across multiple data types, such as text, images, audio, and video.
They have rapidly become ubiquitous across all fields of science and excel at producing rapid results to general inquiries via text or image prompts. 
Several high-quality models have recently become available to the public, such as OpenAI's GPT-4o\footnote{https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-4o} and Google's Gemini-2.5\footnote{https://ai.google.dev/models}.
% \todo{HF: Disclose which snapshot of the models we use for experiments, e.g., \texttt{gpt-4o-\allowbreak2024-08-\allowbreak06} vs. \texttt{gpt-4o} JM: We'll do it in the experiment description}
These models seem to achieve a high level of understanding of several topics, capable of matching or exceeding human-subject performance in the SAT and LSAT exams, as well as competitive programming challenges~\cite{Kishky25}. 
% \todo{Add citations.} 

With the advance of the vision capabilities of these models, a natural question to ask is what exactly are their capabilities and limitations in understanding visualizations? %Recently, there has been interest in 
Recent experiments testing the general visualization understanding of ChatGPT-4 and Gemini, using the Visualization Literacy Assessment Test~\cite{hong2025llms,DBLP:journals/tvcg/BendeckS25}, revealed that both models can handle tasks such as comparing scatterplots or finding correlations fairly well. However, neither model performed consistently well on other tasks, such as reading pie charts or histograms.  

What remains unclear is whether these models have a sense of perception to understand and interpret complex visualizations such as node-link diagrams of networks. 
% Networks can have arbitrarily node-link representations. 
Networks represent complex structures and there can be many different layouts of the same underlying data. 
%that are useful for a given network data.
Hence, there are many possible features of a network that one might want to emphasize, and many techniques to produce them have been proposed. For example, one can highlight local~\cite{DBLP:journals/tvcg/ZhuCHHLZ21,DBLP:journals/cgf/KruigerRMKKT17} or global~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/GansnerKN04} structures, clusters~\cite{DBLP:journals/jgaa/Noack07}, or even particular network properties of interest~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/ForsterKDE0KLMM24}. 
Evaluating or comparing techniques using established quality metrics such as \textit{stress} is common. 
Informally, stress measures how well the underlying shortest-path distances between pairs of nodes are captured in the layout of the network. 
%the pairwise difference between topological distance and distance in the drawing. 
% \todo{Do we want to change graph to network here?}
Lower stress indicates a better representation of the network topology, and recently, an empirical study~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024} determined that human experts can reliably decide which of two diagrams has lower stress and non-experts can be trained to do so, too.
Computing the stress values of node-link diagrams directly 
is difficult if only the diagram is available, for example if the underlying data comes from an online image.
%images but not the full data is available and 
As asking human experts to compare diagrams with respect to stress is expensive, it may be useful to delegate this task to an MLLM.


The research landscape is rapidly evolving with regard to MLLMs, and so it is imperative to broaden our collective understanding as a community of how these models might be used to draw conclusions about visualizations, and how valid these conclusions might be. Additionally, MLLMs show promising value to visualization designers and researchers by potentially allowing thorough, inexpensive piloting for human-subject studies if they indeed possess human-like visualization literacy. We aim to begin to address these questions in the context of network visualization, a yet unexplored topic at the time of writing.

This paper evaluates how MLLMs interpret, perceive, and respond to the most common network visualizations -- node-link diagrams.
% \todo{HF: I feel we should also state a goal for letting LLMs perform these tasks. Is it that we want to replace users in studies? Is the goal to evaluate if MLLMs have network visualization literacy?}.
Specifically, we compare the performance of ~\chatgpt %\todo{CC: We use ``GPT-4o'' rather than ``ChatGPT-4o'' especially for API. Do you want to update the whole paper?}
~and ~\gemini ~by replicating a ~\humansubject ~experiment of Mooney et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024} where participants were given two images of node-link diagrams.
The participants had to decide whether the left or right image showed a node-link diagram with lower stress, or whether both had similar stress.
Our results show that by giving the same training information, MLLMs mimic the performance of all three different types of human participants (untrained novices, trained novices, and experts). % can perform similarly or better than human participants. 
 By fine-tuning the prompts, we obtain better performance with interesting deviations.
% By fine-tuning the prompts we can easily reach performance exceeding experts.
We extract and analyze the main topics from the LLMs' reasonings
%\todo{HF: Is reasoning trace the correct word here?SK: yes, that's the terminology.}
%Furthermore,
and investigate correlations  with the underlying properties of the network layouts.
The reasonings of both MLLM models show that, similar to human participants, they rely on visual proxies when judging stress. 
%
In summary, our contributions are 
\begin{itemize}
    \item the first, to our knowledge, replication of a network visualization human-subject study with MLLM participants,
    \item statistical analysis of MLLM performance compared to human-subject performance,
    \item analysis of how deviating from the instructions given to human-subjects affects the performance of the models, and
    \item an investigation of the reasonings provided by the models for the visualization comparisons performed, and how these responses are affected by network layout properties.
\end{itemize}

All prompts, code, data, results, and analysis are provided in an open-source OSF repository: \url{https://osf.io/748mx/}. We also provide a summary video in supplemental material. 


