\subsection{Background}
\label{sec:background}

Networks arise naturally as a mathematical model of relational systems, such as biological protein interactions~\cite{gao2023hierarchical}, power and infrastructure~\cite{DBLP:journals/nms/Dijck21}, and social interactions~\cite{pascual2022social}. Creating effective network visualizations to understand and interpret such complex systems is a core visualization topic.% highly relevant topic. 

By far the most popular choice of idiom for network visualizations is the so-called \textit{node-link diagram}. In this setting, each entity in the network (called node or vertex) is encoded as a mark, such as a circle, while the presence of a relationship (called link or edge) between two nodes is encoded as a line segment between them. Evaluation of the effectiveness of node-link diagrams has historically been grounded in perceptual principles~\cite{DBLP:journals/vlc/Purchase02} with studies showing the aesthetic metrics of edge crossings, angular resolution, node uniformity, edge length uniformity, and several others to affect readability and task performance~\cite{DBLP:journals/ese/PurchaseCA02}. 

As there are many possible features of a network that one might want to emphasize in a node-link diagram, many techniques to produce them have been proposed. 
Some techniques highlight local structures~\cite{DBLP:journals/tvcg/ZhuCHHLZ21,DBLP:journals/cgf/KruigerRMKKT17}, which preserve small neighborhoods around nodes but often distort the large-scale structures of a network. 
Other techniques highlight global structures~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/GansnerKN04}, which attempt to capture the large-scale structures of a network while allowing local errors. %neighborhoods resulting in the ``hairball'' effect for dense networks.
Techniques can also highlight clusters by forcing separation between groups of nodes~\cite{DBLP:journals/jgaa/Noack07}, or 
to ``visually prove'' specific network properties, such as the existence of a bridge in the network~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/ForsterKDE0KLMM24}.
% For example\todo{rephrase giving more details here}, one can highlight local~\cite{DBLP:journals/tvcg/ZhuCHHLZ21,DBLP:journals/cgf/KruigerRMKKT17} or global~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/GansnerKN04} structures, clusters~\cite{DBLP:journals/jgaa/Noack07}, or even particular network properties of interest~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/ForsterKDE0KLMM24}.
It is common to evaluate or compare techniques using established quality (faithfulness) metrics~\cite{DBLP:conf/apvis/NguyenEH13}, which express the distortion of a network property represented in the diagram. The most common quality metric of this type is known as \textit{stress},
% shown by Di Bartolomeo et al.~\cite{DBLP:journals/cgf/BartolomeoCSPWD24} as
the third most reported quantitative evaluation metric in network visualization (behind running time and the number of crossings)~\cite{DBLP:journals/cgf/BartolomeoCSPWD24}. 


Stress is a family of quality metrics that measures the discrepancy between the graph-theoretic distances between nodes in the data (i.e., the number of edges on a shortest path between them), and the realized geometric distances between their coordinates in the diagram. The most often used stress measure is called normalized stress,  defined by Gansner et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/GansnerKN04}. 
% as 
% \[\sigma(X) = \frac{\left( ||X_i - X_j|| - d_{i,j}\right)^2}{d_{i,j}^2}\]
% where, in the given node-link diagram, $X_i$ is the position of node $i$, $X_j$~is the position of node $j$, and $d_{i,j}$ is the graph-theoretic (shortest path) distance between node $i$ and node~$j$. 
%
The concept of stress dates back to the works of Torgerson~\cite{torgerson1952multidimensional}, Kruskal~\cite{kruskal1964multidimensional}, Shepard~\cite{shepard1962analysis}, and later Sammon~\cite{DBLP:journals/tc/Sammon69} who initially utilize it as a statistical analysis tool. It was first used in network visualization as an optimization function proposed by Kamada and Kawai~\cite{DBLP:journals/ipl/KamadaK89}. Their technique was later improved with Gansner et al.'s stress majorization~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/GansnerKN04}, and Zheng et al.~\cite{DBLP:journals/tvcg/ZhengPG19} show that stochastic gradient descent is even more effective at optimizing stress. There are many variants of stress used as optimization functions~\cite{DBLP:journals/tvcg/GansnerHN13,DBLP:conf/gd/MillerHK23}, and many more examples of works which use stress as an evaluation metric despite not directly optimizing it~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/HongETWCHLC19,DBLP:conf/gd/MarnerSTKEH14,DBLP:journals/cgf/WageningenMT24,DBLP:conf/gd/SimonettoAK17,DBLP:journals/cgf/ArleoMA22}. 

Stress has been shown to be a good proxy for symmetry~\cite{DBLP:journals/cgf/WelchK17}, and it has been shown that people tend to prefer diagrams with lower stress~\cite{chimani2014people}. 
%However, normalized stress has several issues as it is defined. It is sensitive to the size of the drawing coordinates, and is not bounded to a finite interpretable range~\cite{DBLP:conf/beliv/Smelser0K24}. 
A recent study of Mooney et al.\ attempts to answer the broad question, ``Can people see stress?~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024}'' 
% The study is conducted on three groups of participants: untrained novices, trained novices, and experts. 
The study is further detailed in \cref{sec:human-exp}, but a key takeaway is that participants could reliably identify diagrams with lower stress, although the feedback received and subsequent interviews conducted indicated that the participants were making this judgement on a perceptual level. In other words, although participants were asked directly about stress, they relied on high-level visual proxies to identify the stress of a diagram. In this work, we primarily ask whether these results extend to MLLMs and to what extent. If asked directly about stress, which has a well-defined mathematical definition, will an MLLM also engage in a similar high-level perceptual processing as human participants indicated?
