\subsection{Research Question}

Our experiment tries to shine a light on the following research questions: 

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ1}: Given the same information as ~\humanparticipants, how effectively do ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~identify lower-stress node-link diagram compared to human performance?
    \item \textbf{RQ2}: What performance can we expect by carefully engineering the prompts?
    \item \textbf{RQ3}: Can we obtain any insight on the decision-making process of ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini?
    \item \textbf{RQ4}: Is there a relationship between the properties of diagrams
    and the given answers by both MLLM models?  
\end{itemize}

In \textbf{RQ1}, we focus on replicating the experiment by Mooney et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024}. 
However, instead of ~\humanparticipants, ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~perform the study. This entails giving the same relevant study information and training examples as prompts to both MLLM models. We then focus on three aspects: firstly, we investigate how the performance differs between ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini. Secondly, as different levels of training were used in the original experiment~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024}, we investigate how this impacts the performance of ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~when given the same information. Thirdly, we compare the performance of the MLLM models to the performance of the ~\humanparticipants.

In \textbf{RQ2}, we answer if it is possible to increase the performance of the MLLM models when we carefully engineer the prompts with more appropriate information than in \textbf{RQ1}. 

In \textbf{RQ3}, we shift over to the reasonings the models provide together with their answers. We investigate what visual cues the models use to infer an answer. Also, we try to find similarities between the answers of both models and the answers provided by ~\humanparticipants. Lastly, for \textbf{RQ4}, we perform a statistical analysis between the properties of the diagrams and the given answers. Specifically, we focus on properties mentioned in \textbf{RQ3}.


