\subsection{Tuning the Prompts}
\label{sec:prompt-engineering}

While the prompts from \cref{sec:prompts} focus on being as close as possible to the instructions from the ~\humansubject ~experiment, they might be suboptimal for an MLLM.
Hence, we focused on improving the performance by tuning a prompt for better performance. We show snippets from the prompt and explain the overall strategy. The full prompt can be found in the supplemental material.

Several strategies and recommendations~\cite{reynolds2021prompt, openai} for better prompts have been proposed. Our prompt is based on the ``chain of thought'' strategy~\cite{Wei0SBIXCLZ22}, where the structure of instructions is fine-grained and provides clear delimiters between sections. After several iterations, we present the \textbf{Tuned} prompt with the highest overall accuracy:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Provide a concise role for the MLLM and what information it will receive.
    \item Carefully explain stress and how it can be visually evaluated.
    \item Lay out the evaluation criteria, how they can be inferred, and which visual cues help during the evaluation process. Specifically, we focus on node distribution, edge lengths, and crossings.
    \item Detailed instructions on how the evaluation process works.
    \item Show the MLLM training examples.
\end{itemize}

The first two bullet points are aimed to set the context for the MLLM. We find that the models understand the concept of stress well, and it is debatable if it is necessary in this context. However, this might be required in other domains to achieve good results. 

From our experience, we find that laying out detailed evaluation criteria, a structured evaluation process, and fitting examples had the most impact on performance. 
Our \tuned prompt defines the most obvious visual proxies to determine stress and tries to guide the models toward a correct answer. 
