We considered conducting traditional difference-of-means tests (i.e.  $t$- or Wilcoxon tests), but this would require either comparing aggregate human-subject accuracy against individual MLLM responses or arbitrarily aggregating independent MLLM accuracies. The first approach is unfair in favor of human responses which are aggregated over several trials, and the second approach introduces structure that is not present. For these reasons, we quickly discarded these analysis methods. 

We instead employ a bootstrapping approach~\cite{tibshirani1993introduction}, which is a general method for statistical comparison and has been used in many visualization studies~\cite{hong2025llms,dragicevic2016fair,brehmer2018visualizing,tang2024comparative}.
% \todo{JM: remember to find more examples. CC: Agree, need some qualitative analysis especially for low-contrast street, and comparison between human \& MLLM}.
The bootstrap statistical analysis method takes a data collection and creates many thousands of simulated samples (of the same size as the original) by drawing from the original data collection with replacement. On each of these simulated samples one can compute statistics of interest, typically the mean or median, which creates a distribution of statistics. Given the large number of statistics, the resulting confidence intervals are reliable. The only assumption required of the original data is that it accurately represents the true population it is sampled from.

So, to analyze the difference between the MLLM and human-subject groups, we treat their individual responses as sample units which results in nine total samples: three for each context of \trained, \untrained, \expert, and another three for each agent: ~\chatgpt, ~\gemini, and ~\humanparticipants. For all analyses, we perform 10,000 resamples. 

% human-subjects.

\paragraph{Results}

% To answer \textbf{RQ1}, \textit{How do~\chatgpt and~\gemini compare to human-subjects in identifying a lower stress drawing?} 
To answer this, we conduct a difference of means test between comparable groups -- ~\human ~with ~\chatgpt ~and ~\human ~with ~\gemini. We do this for the \trained, \untrained, and \expert study settings.  

The confidence intervals of the test are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:rq1_DoM}. 
% From the three settings, we see one case for every possibility of difference between human-subjects and~\chatgpt. 
In the \expert setting (least context),
% In the setting with the least context provided, the expert setting, 
the human \expert participants outperform both MLLMs by about 7\%; this result is statistically significant in both cases. Notably, the \expert participants were provided just as much context as the MLLMs. 
% little to no context on the task or network visualization. 

In the setting with a moderate amount of context, the \untrained setting, we see the opposite result with regards to both MLLMs. Here, ~\chatgpt  ~outperforms \untrained ~\humanparticipants ~by around 8\% while ~\gemini ~outperforms by nearly 15\%; these results are statistically significant. 
In the Mooney et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024} experiment, the \untrained participants were novices given some information but no training. This seems to indicate that MLLMs can perform well given information and manual training is not as necessary as it is for human-subject participants.

% In the setting with the most amount of context, the trained setting, 
Finally, in the \trained setting, participants were given the most amount of context. We see that while ~\chatgpt ~achieves around 3\% more accuracy than \trained ~~\humanparticipants ~on average, the result is not significant. The~\gemini model achieves 7\% more accuracy and this is a significant result. Looking overall, it appears that ~\chatgpt ~performs similarly to ~\humansubjects ~overall in terms of accuracy on identifying stress. 

\paragraph{Discussion}
From our results, we draw several conclusions. Overall, MLLMs are competitive with ~\humanparticipants ~in identifying lower stress network diagrams. Even in the worst performing case for MLLMs (\expert ~~\gemini), the lowest mean accuracy we see after bootstrapping is around 60\%. This is not so different from the lowest ~\humanparticipant ~mean accuracy of 67\% (\untrained). However, it is clear that the information, context, and prompt provided to an MLLM makes a significant effect in the accuracy. 

Specifically, the \expert setting for MLLMs performs relatively poorly, being more comparable to \untrained ~~\humanparticipants. This could be for any number of reasons, for instance the MLLMs may be relying on their prior knowledge that doesn't apply such as the colloquial definition of stress. We investigate the MLLM reasonings in more detail in \cref{sec:RQ3}. Regardless, it is clear that both MLLM models cannot perform as an \expert without more instruction. 


%\todo{CC: Should we move it to the discussion section which also mentions this. \\ JM: I think it is relevant here, as a takeaway from our RQ1 analysis.}
We believe it is generally ill-advised to fully replace human subjects in visualization evaluation studies. % wholesale.
The kinds of training and instructions given to an MLLM affect the performance in statistically significant ways compared to human-subjects. For instance, the \trained MLLMs perform more similarly to \expert human participants than they do to \trained human participants. However, MLLMs do show promise in serving as helpful tools for pilot studies to generate quick feedback, check for floor and ceiling effects, and gauge difficulty.
