In order to analyze the differences between the ``\expert reasoning'' of both models more carefully, we labeled the text responses of ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~in the \expert setting according to the reasoning themes used; see \cref{tab:expert-themes}. In the \expert setting, prompts contain neither the definition of stress nor references to proxies, i.e., we can expect that MLLMs provide reasonings based on their native interpretation of network diagrams. The most noteworthy differences between both MLLMs are the following. First, ~\chatgpt ~responses almost always contain references to the number of crossings, whereas ~\gemini ~mentions this only half as often
%slightly more carefully in 57.4\% of all reasonings 
(this accounts for each reasoning using at least one of the themes ``Few Crossings'', ``Equal Distribution of Crossings'' and ``Many Crossings''). On the other hand, ~\gemini ~refers to proxies measuring edge length deviation more often than ~\chatgpt. To this end it is noteworthy that edge length deviation correlates strongly with stress as we will discuss in the next section. While both models report clusters of nodes in dense areas as an obstruction for low stress in roughly half of the responses, curiously both also mention it sometimes as a helpful feature for reducing stress. ~\gemini ~does that more often in more than 10\% of all its responses. Both models also address several other readability related properties.
% , most often, 
Both models most often mention visual clutter, readability and visual complexity; the remaining such topics are distinct between both models. Finally, we also remark that in three cases, ~\gemini ~provided a reasoning contradicting its chosen answer, e.g., it chose the ``left'' image by choosing Image 1,
% stating the numerical value 1 
but then provided a reasoning stating ``\emph{The drawing on the left has a more centralized structure, with many edges converging on a smaller number of nodes. This creates a sense of congestion and visual stress.}''.  




\paragraph{Discussion} Both ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~discuss visual proxies correlated with stress in their reasonings --  node distribution, edge length deviation, and number of edge crossings. 
% Interestingly, 
This happens even in the \textbf{Expert} setting where the definition of stress is not provided. 

%While in the \textbf{Expert} responses the term ``visual stress'' is a frequent reasoning pattern for both \chatgpt and \gemini, both models prefer to use the term to conclude sentences rather than to reason about the quality of the diagram, e.g., by saying \emph{``Image 2 has more edge crossings and a denser cluster of nodes in the center, which can increase visual stress.''} ( \chatgpt ) or ``\emph{The image on the right has a more concentrated area of nodes and edges, which creates a higher sense of visual stress.}'' ( \gemini ). Thus, the \textbf{Expert} setting appears to shed insights into how \chatgpt and \gemini assess the overall quality of a visualization according to visual proxies they were given during training.

The two models appear to value aesthetic criteria differently in the \expert setting where the least amount of additional information is provided via prompts. ~\chatgpt ~ focuses on the presence of crossings, followed  closely by node distribution. In contrast, ~\gemini ~mentions reasons related to node distribution more often than reasons related to the number of crossings and also considers the edge length deviation in 26.9\% of all cases compared to 5.6\% for ~\chatgpt. 
%It is also noteworthy that the language level changed quite drastically when telling the models to act as experts.

Only when provided with a definition (in conditions \untrained and \trained),  both ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~consistently refer to patterns related to the definition of stress (``path lengths''/``shortest paths'', ``distance nodes''/``proportional'', ``distances between nodes'').
In contrast, in the \expert and \tuned setting,  both models often refer to the term ``{visual stress}''. 
%which is part of the query. 
%As with the term ``Visual Stress'' in the \textbf{Expert} setting, 
Proportionality between distances and lengths of shortest paths are often used as endings of sentences by most models, e.g., by saying \emph{``Image 1 appears to have a more even distribution of nodes and edges, suggesting that the distances between nodes are more proportional to the shortest paths.''} ( ~\chatgpt ) or \emph{``Image 1 has a more uniform distribution of nodes and edge lengths, suggesting a better correspondence between distance and path length, hence lower stress.''} ( ~\gemini ). Given that the MLLMs are not allowed to execute code 
(verified by API limitations), it is unlikely that they in fact computed stress according to the definition and this rather indicates that both models are eager to express that they understood the assignment. 

Overall,  we can extract insights on the decision-making process but careful prompting and analysis are required as both models tend to mention phrases provided in prompts even if they are not used in the actual decision process.

