\subsection{RQ4}\label{sec:RQ4}
We start our analysis of RQ4 by establishing whether there is a correlation between stress and three other diagram properties. 
Specifically, we consider node uniformity, edge length deviation and crossings, as visual proxies for stress.
%the properties we investigate as visual proxies.

\begin{quote}
\begin{mdframed}[roundcorner=10pt]
    {\bf RQ4:} Is there a relationship between the properties of diagrams and the given answers by both MLLM models?
\end{mdframed}
\end{quote}

\paragraph{Results}
 Node uniformity~\cite{TaylorR05} is calculated by assuming a $\sqrt{|V|} \times \sqrt{|V|}$ grid over the diagram and computing the spread of nodes. 
A value of 1 indicates all nodes are in one cell, while 0 indicates uniform distribution.
Edge length deviation~\cite{AhmedLDKL22} is the standard deviation from the  average edge length. 
Edge crossings~\cite{PurchaseCJ95} is a number between 0 and 1, corresponding to the number of crossings in the diagram divided by the maximum possible (when every edge crosses every other edge).
% $|E|(|E| - 1)$.
These three properties consistently appear as visual proxies in the answers provided by the MLLMs in \cref{sec:RQ3} and are frequently mentioned by the participants in the ~\humansubject ~stress-perception experiment~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024}.
%Furthermore, we conjecture that evaluating node uniformity, edge length, and crossings is easier than the pairwise stress ($|V|^2$). 
%considered proxies for humans to evaluate stress in a drawing~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024}.

We first perform a Spearman correlation test on all networks in our dataset showing a significant correlation ($p < 0.01$) between stress and all three proxies. 
Both edge lengths ($\rho = 0.76$) and node uniformity ($\rho = 0.70$) strongly correlate with stress, while crossings ($\rho=0.58$) have a high-moderate correlation; see 
\cref{fig:correlation_graph_metrics} for a correlation heatmap. 
