We conducted our analysis using logistic regression. 
Here, we focus on the \trained prompt and report the detailed statistical results, including regression coefficients, exact correlations, and significance tests for all other prompts in the supplemental material.
Our analysis examines how the three proxies predict the output of a model for a given prompt.
Individually, each proxy shows a statistically significant relationship with the outcome for both models. 
Edge length deviation emerges as the strongest individual predictor (pseudo $R^2 \approx 0.37$–$0.38$), closely followed by node uniformity (pseudo $R^2 \approx 0.32$-$0.33$), and then crossings
 %, which contributes somewhat less explanatory power 
(pseudo $R^2 \approx 0.09$–$0.13$).

Combining all three proxies into a multiple logistic regression model, each remains highly significant (all $p < 0.001$). The combined model substantially improves predictive strength, achieving a pseudo $R^2$ between $0.51$ and $0.55$, clearly surpassing the explanatory power of any single-variable model. 
%Regarding concerns about correlation,
Node uniformity and edge length deviation correlate with each other ($\rho \approx 0.73$–$0.74$, $p < 0.001$), but each variable independently contributes meaningful explanatory information.

Analyzing both ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~consistently shows that a combined logistic regression model with all three proxies provides superior predictive capability. 
Minor numerical differences between the analyses of the models do not affect this core conclusion. 

