We emphasize that our experiments do not directly aim to draw~new conclusions on the human perception of network visualizations. However, our results may provide early evidence that MLLMs may indeed be applicable in studies on the aesthetics of (relational) data visualizations, with some caveats. 
%In fact, in our own work replicating the study of Mooney et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024} 
For example, the MLLM models consistently underused the option, ``the stress is the same''. The use of the word, \enquote{same} is a potentially confounding factor as it is slightly misleading; two network diagrams are very unlikely to have the exact same stress values. However, two diagrams are considered to have the \enquote{same} stress if they are within a small threshold. Had an MLLM been used as a pilot test subject in this study, this  wording issue might have been caught. 

%It is worth remarking that MLLMs do not always manage to match human-subject performance. In particular, sometimes MLLMs manage to outperform human study participants~\cite{DBLP:journals/coling/ZiemsHSCZY24}. We remark that this may pose a critical danger to crowd-sourced self-reported user studies because LLMs may already be participating without the researchers' knowledge~\cite{DBLP:conf/chi/HamalainenTK23,DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2306-07899}. Assessing the capabilities of MLLMs in a controlled manner may be a key ingredient in understanding and reducing such risks, and is a good avenue for future work.
% \todo[inline]{the last two paragraphs are not really "related work" but "discussion" or "limitations" topics...}

Our study has several limitations, starting with the use of only two of the many available MLLMs. Further, both ~\chatgpt ~and ~\gemini ~are closed-source, making study replication difficult as the models are phased out. 
We investigate only one high-level perceptual task. While we investigate this task in great detail, it does not tell us much about performance of other specific tasks on complex visualizations. We note that evaluating the stress of a node-link diagram involves several low-level tasks to compare exactly (path following, distance estimation, deriving value, etc.) or high-level perceptual processes (estimating symmetry, distribution of points and lines). Finally, although we attempt to replicate the ~\humansubject ~study of Mooney et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/MooneyPWK024} as precisely as possible with MLLMs, there are small differences in how the data is given and collected (e.g., every request to the MLLM is independent). 
%A study designed explicitly to compare human and MLLM participants may allow for increased interpretability of results. 

There are many interesting avenues for future work involving MLLMs and node-link diagrams.
